\begin{abstract}
A network may need an early cue long after the neurons that received it have fired. In a leaky integrate-and-fire neuron, the cue and each subsequent reset contribute to the same voltage trace. We introduce SPRiF, a five-state neuron that stores input current in a spectral memory and resets the membrane that reads it. One real decay and a damped rotation provide the temporal basis. Two learned projections drive the membrane, one immediately and one through an auxiliary filter delayed by one step. We derive the membrane drive in terms of the input history and identify the additional spike-history term produced by a reset along the memory input direction. We evaluate SPRiF on five temporal benchmarks and test reset placement and mode organization with matched controls on two of them. Preserving memory at reset gave mean accuracy gains of 3.37 percentage points on permuted sequential MNIST and 4.56 on Spiking Heidelberg Digits over the memory-reset control. On delayed match-to-sample, forcing spikes at 40 timesteps of an unseen 2500-ms delay yielded 84.41\% accuracy after clean training and 99.72\% after training with at most eight intervention timesteps.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Spiking neural networks, temporal processing, spectral memory, neuron dynamics, scalar reset.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

\IEEEPARstart{I}{n} a delayed decision, the first cue may be gone by the time the second arrives. Spikes emitted between the cues serve two roles in a recurrent spiking neural network (SNN). They reset the membranes that fired and enter the recurrent current on the next step. In the leaky integrate-and-fire (LIF) model, an input pulse and a subsequent reset leave traces in the same membrane voltage, both governed by the same decay. The signal available at the second cue therefore depends on the intervening spikes as well as the first cue.

A neuron with several state variables can assign these roles differently. Adaptive thresholds retain a history of activity~\cite{asrnn,adlif}, resonant dynamics produce lag-dependent responses~\cite{izhikevich_rf,brf,drf}, and coupled compartments distribute integration across states~\cite{tclif,dhsnn}. PMSN separates linear hidden compartments from a firing compartment~\cite{pmsn2026}, while DMP-SNN maintains a memory shared across a layer~\cite{sun2026dmp}. Our question concerns the organization of a fixed number of states within one neuron. Which temporal responses can the membrane read, and how does a spike change those responses?

SPRiF assigns three coordinates to a spectral memory, one to a filter and one to the membrane. A pulse excites a decaying trace and a damped rotation. The membrane reads a projection of the current spectral state and a filtered projection from the preceding step. These two paths allow the neuron to change the weight of older inputs while holding the instantaneous response fixed. The local reset acts on the membrane alone. For a specified current sequence, we derive the kernel that maps input to membrane drive and identify the extra spike-history term introduced when reset also acts on the spectral state.

We evaluate SPRiF on five temporal benchmarks and test the two design choices on permuted sequential MNIST (PS-MNIST) and Spiking Heidelberg Digits (SHD). Two controls retain the same five states, eleven learned local coefficients and layer widths. One adds a reset to spectral memory. The other replaces the spectral block with three driven real decays. A second experiment, Spike-Intervention Delayed Match-to-Sample (SI-DMS), forces spikes between two cues. It tests whether the full recurrent network can still compare the cues when its spike history changes.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

\subsection{States That Carry Temporal Information}

Adaptive neurons such as ASRNN and adLIF retain activity in a variable that influences later firing~\cite{asrnn,adlif}. In resonant neurons, the phase of a coupled state also affects the response to a past input~\cite{izhikevich_rf,brf}. TC-LIF couples dendritic and somatic states~\cite{tclif}, while dendritic heterogeneity gives different branches different time constants~\cite{dhsnn}. A close relative of SPRiF is D-RF, whose resonant dendrites feed a somatic readout and whose threshold depends on spike history~\cite{drf}. Its dendrites also evolve without subtractive output reset. SPRiF combines a real decay with a rotational pair and gives the membrane two projections of those states. The second projection acts through its own filter one step later.

\subsection{Spectral Memory and Output Reset}

PMSN separates the dynamics of its hidden compartments from discharge at its output~\cite{pmsn2026}. Unrolling its diagonalized hidden transition gives a convolution from injected current to the drive at the output compartment. Its parallel output update requires a separate construction, temporal-decoupled reset, which operates on rectified current and discharges integer multiples of the threshold. SPRiF specifies the poles of the hidden transition directly and uses a leaky membrane with signed drive and one threshold subtraction per binary spike. This choice gives a closed form for the drive to the membrane while retaining a sequential firing decision. DMP-SNN makes a different architectural choice by sharing memory within a layer~\cite{sun2026dmp}, and SpikingSSMs couple state-space sequence processing to sparse spiking outputs~\cite{spikingssms}.

\subsection{Spike History in Computation and Training}

NiSNN-A writes LIF voltage as the difference between accumulated input and accumulated reset~\cite{nisnna2025}. Its NiLIF update estimates the unknown spike train from the input-only accumulation to obtain a noniterative computation. In SPRiF, spikes are generated by thresholding the actual membrane candidate at each step. The local response to a realized spike can then be derived from the state equations.

IML-Spikeformer uses input-dependent thresholds for multilevel firing and examines the resulting firing-rate distributions~\cite{imlspikeformer2026}. STDL changes which dependencies are retained during learning to fit a training-memory budget~\cite{stdl2026}. In the comparisons below we keep surrogate-gradient backpropagation through time (BPTT) and change the forward dynamics of the neuron.

\section{SPRiF Neuron: Model and Analysis}
\label{sec:model}

\subsection{From a Reset-Coupled Trace to Separate State Roles}
\label{sec:motivation}

To fix the timing convention, let $v_t$ be a LIF membrane immediately after the current reset. Its candidate, spike, and stored voltage are
\begin{equation}
\widetilde v_t=\beta v_{t-1}+I_t,\qquad
z_t=H(\widetilde v_t-\theta),\qquad
v_t=\widetilde v_t-\theta z_t,
\label{eq:lif}
\end{equation}
where $0<\beta<1$, $\theta>0$, and $H(a)=1$ for $a\geq0$ and zero otherwise. Unrolling gives
\begin{equation}
v_t=\beta^{t+1}v_{-1}
+\sum_{j=0}^{t}\beta^{t-j} I_j
-\theta\sum_{j=0}^{t}\beta^{t-j}z_j.
\label{eq:lif_unrolled}
\end{equation}
A unit of current at step $j$ contributes $\beta^{t-j}$ at step $t$. A spike at the same step contributes $-\theta\beta^{t-j}$. The two contributions differ in sign and scale but have the same time course. SPRiF retains membrane reset and gives the membrane access to a second history, a filtered record of the input current whose coordinates are preserved at discharge.

\subsection{Spectral Memory and Membrane Update}
\label{sec:forward}

For an input vector $\mathbf{s}_t$ and a recurrent spiking layer, the current vector is
\begin{equation}
\mathbf I_t=W_{\mathrm{in}}\mathbf s_t+
W_{\mathrm{rec}}\mathbf z_{t-1}+\mathbf b.
\label{eq:current}
\end{equation}
The recurrent term is omitted for a feedforward layer. The equations below concern one neuron and suppress its index. Its current $I_t$ is the corresponding component of $\mathbf I_t$, and it stores $\mathbf x_t\in\R^3$, $h_t\in\R$, and $u_t^0\in\R$:
\begin{equation}
\left\{
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf x_t&=A\mathbf x_{t-1}+BI_t,\\
h_t&=\eta_1h_{t-1}+\bar{\mathbf g}_1^\top\mathbf x_t,\\
\widetilde u_t^0&=\eta_0u_{t-1}^0+h_{t-1}
                    +\mathbf g_0^\top\mathbf x_t,\\
z_t&=H(\widetilde u_t^0-\theta),\\
u_t^0&=\widetilde u_t^0-\theta z_t.
\end{aligned}
\right.
\label{eq:sprif}
\end{equation}
The transition and input vector are
\begin{equation}
A=\begin{bmatrix}
\alpha&0&0\\
0&\rho\cos\omega&-\rho\sin\omega\\
0&\rho\sin\omega&\rho\cos\omega
\end{bmatrix},
\qquad
B=\begin{bmatrix}1-\alpha\\1-\rho\\0\end{bmatrix}.
\label{eq:AB}
\end{equation}
The two directly learned readouts form
\begin{equation}
\bar G=
\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf g_0^\top\\\bar{\mathbf g}_1^\top\end{bmatrix}
\in\R^{2\times3}.
\label{eq:G}
\end{equation}
For finite raw parameters, the constraints are enforced by
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\alpha&=\sigma(\alpha_{\rm raw}),&
\rho&=\sigma(\rho_{\rm raw}),\\
\omega&=\pi\sigma(\omega_{\rm raw}),&
\eta_i&=\sigma(\eta_{i,{\rm raw}}),\quad i\in\{0,1\}.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:constraints}
\end{equation}
Here $\sigma$ is the logistic sigmoid. Thus $\alpha,\rho,\eta_i\in(0,1)$ and $\omega\in(0,\pi)$. The six entries of $\bar G$ are unconstrained real coefficients. The threshold is a fixed task setting.

Figure~\ref{fig:organization} contrasts the state organization of LIF and SPRiF. In SPRiF, two routes connect $\mathbf x_t$ to firing. The direct projection enters $\widetilde u_t^0$ immediately. The second projection updates $h_t$, which first enters the membrane at $t+1$. At the current step the membrane reads $h_{t-1}$. All intrinsic states start at zero for an independent sequence.

