\subsection{SI-DMS: Task and Spike Intervention}
\label{sec:sidms}

SI-DMS is a binary match/non-match task. Each trial presents two 100-ms cues separated by a delay, following a 50-ms baseline. Thirty input channels encode the left cue, right cue and background in groups of ten. At each 5-ms step, active cue channels emit a Bernoulli spike with probability 0.20 and background channels with probability 0.05. A recurrent layer of 64 neurons drives two leaky outputs with a 20-ms time constant. The final outputs determine the class.

The training delays are 200, 400, 800 and 1600 ms. Clean training uses natural firing throughout. Intervention training samples $K\in\{0,1,2,4,8\}$ forced-spike times during the delay. At each selected time, ten of the 64 units (nominally 15\%) have their membrane candidates set to the current threshold plus 0.05. The induced spikes trigger each model's reset and enter the recurrent feedback. For SPRiF, the overwrite leaves the current spectral and auxiliary states intact. Training uses Adam with learning rate 0.003, batches of 256 and 3000 updates. Stress evaluation uses $K=40$, beyond the training range, and adds a 2500-ms delay.

For seed $s$, delay $d$, count $K$, and fraction $f$, the paired loss is
\begin{equation}
\Delta_{s,d}(K,f)=
\Acc_{s,d}(0)-\Acc_{s,d}(K,f).
\label{eq:paired_loss}
\end{equation}
At $K=40$ and nominal $f=0.15$, we report the loss in percentage points over all five evaluation delays and separately at 2500~ms.

\subsection{Statistical Reporting}
\label{sec:statistics}

One training run is one replicate. We report mean accuracy and sample standard deviation across seeds $\{0,1,2,42,1111\}$. Published entries retain their original reporting conventions. In SI-DMS, each delay has equal weight within a seed. We report the mean $\Delta_{40}$, equal to mean clean accuracy minus mean stressed accuracy.

\section{Results and Discussion}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Temporal Classification at the Reported Network Sizes}

Table~\ref{tab:main_results} summarizes five temporal benchmarks. On PS-MNIST, SPRiF reached $95.86\pm0.14\%$ with 0.067 million parameters, compared with $95.41\pm0.52\%$ for the native DMP-SNN rerun at the same reported size. On SHD, SPRiF reached $91.66\pm0.42\%$ with 0.05 million parameters. The BRF and TC-LIF reruns reached $91.28\pm0.53\%$ and $88.73\pm0.44\%$ with 0.11 and 0.14 million parameters, respectively. The controls below test how SPRiF uses its states on these two tasks while keeping layer width and local capacity fixed.

\subsection{Temporal Responses of the Spectral Memory}
\label{sec:temporal_responses}

Figure~\ref{fig:temporal_kernels} connects the spectral basis in Eq.~\eqref{eq:impulse} to the temporal responses on PS-MNIST, GSC and QTDB. The impulse responses show a real decay alongside damped oscillatory components (Fig.~\ref{fig:temporal_kernels}a). These components provide traces with different durations and phases for the membrane projections in Eq.~\eqref{eq:drive_kernel}. The frequency responses summarize the memory states across neurons in each task (Fig.~\ref{fig:temporal_kernels}b). Their shaded ranges describe variation across neurons. In the slow-decay comparison, the displayed SPRiF responses retain a larger normalized amplitude than ASRNN over the plotted interval on GSC and QTDB (Fig.~\ref{fig:temporal_kernels}c).

