\subsection{Reset Placement and the Choice of Modes}

Figure~\ref{fig:mechanism} shows SPRiF memory states, membrane voltage and spikes around a recorded firing event on PS-MNIST. The memory trajectories continue through the marked event, while the membrane trace reflects firing and reset. This illustrates the separation in Eq.~\eqref{eq:sprif}: the spike changes the stored membrane voltage without subtracting from the spectral memory.

\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.80\columnwidth]{figure/mechanism_composite.pdf}
\caption{SPRiF states before and after a recorded spike on PS-MNIST. The upper panel shows the real and two oscillatory memory coordinates, vertically offset for visibility. The middle panel shows relative membrane voltage, and the lower panel shows the spike train. The vertical dashed line marks the reference spike at relative step zero. The aligned traces show how firing and membrane reset relate to the continuing evolution of the internal memory states.}
\label{fig:mechanism}
\end{figure}

The two neurons have the same scalar membrane reset. In \mr{}, a spike at $t$ also contributes $r[1]=-\theta\mathbf g_0^\top A\widehat B$ to the drive at $t+1$ (Eq.~\eqref{eq:reset_kernel}). The filter first carries part of that displacement to the membrane at $t+2$. Its sign depends on the learned projections. A memory reset thus adds a spike-dependent signal to the readout even if the incoming current is held fixed. SPRiF omits this path and scored 3.37 percentage points higher on PS-MNIST and 4.56 points higher on SHD (Table~\ref{tab:ablation}).

The mode comparison keeps the reset on the membrane. In \rmodes{}, the readout combines three driven exponential decays. In SPRiF, an input also leaves a trace whose direction rotates with elapsed time. Its projection can therefore weight successive phases differently under a common decay envelope (Eq.~\eqref{eq:impulse}). The real-plus-rotational block scored 2.34 percentage points higher on PS-MNIST and 2.47 higher on SHD than the three-real-mode block. This comparison tests the choice of basis and its associated input coupling together.

\subsection{Forced Spikes During a Memory Delay}

SI-DMS forces spikes while the network must retain the first cue. Given the same preceding states and current, a forced discharge in SPRiF leaves the spectral and filter coordinates at their unforced values at that step. The membrane voltage can differ immediately. If the spike pattern changes, neuron $i$ receives an additional spectral input $B_i(W_{\rm rec}\,\delta\mathbf z_t)_i$ on the next step (Eq.~\eqref{eq:recurrent_feedback}). Thus a locally preserved trace can evolve differently over the remainder of the delay.

SPRiF and ASRNN reached $100.00\%$ clean accuracy under both training regimes. After clean training, forcing spikes at 40 delay timesteps gave five-delay accuracies of $84.63\%$ and $62.31\%$, respectively. Training with up to eight intervention timesteps brought the corresponding accuracies to $99.79\%$ and $68.79\%$ under the same stress test (Table~\ref{tab:sidms_results}). For SPRiF, the mean loss relative to clean evaluation narrowed from 15.37 to 0.21 percentage points. At the unseen 2500-ms delay, intervention-trained SPRiF retained $99.72\%$ accuracy.

The memory-reset control had a different starting point. After intervention training, its accuracy went from $88.14\%$ clean to $51.69\%$ under stress, a loss of 36.45 points. This within-model contrast includes the direct memory path identified in Eq.~\eqref{eq:drive_histories}, the membrane overwrite and recurrent feedback. In SPRiF the direct memory path is absent under both training regimes. Its smaller loss after intervention training therefore measures a change in the trained network's response under the same local reset rule.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

SPRiF gives the membrane a decaying trace and a phase-dependent trace of past input, each available through an immediate and a delayed projection. Each spike resets the membrane and can re-enter the spectral pathway one step later through recurrent current. Extending reset to spectral memory adds a distinct spike-history term to the drive. At fixed local capacity, both preserving that memory at discharge and retaining its rotational pair improved mean accuracy on PS-MNIST and SHD. On delayed match-to-sample, the same architecture sustained accurate cue comparison after intervention training, even when both the delay and the number of forced-spike times exceeded the training range.

